WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.062 --> 00:00:10.147
FAYE MOSS: Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History , where we sit down with some of the most famous people from

2
00:00:10.148 --> 00:00:25.712
the past. Today's guest was an artist, sculptor, engineer, scientist, and inventor—in other words, a true Renaissance man—and one who actually lived during the Renaissance.

3
00:00:25.713 --> 00:00:47.629
Please help me welcome Leonardo da Vinci!

4
00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:51.267
LEONARDO DA VINCI: Thank you, Faye. It's lovely

5
00:00:51.268 --> 00:01:01.059
to be here. FAYE MOSS: I have so many things to ask you, Leonardo, that I don't know where to begin. I guess we should start

6
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:11.650
at the beginning! LEONARDO DA VINCI: A genius idea, Faye. I was born in 1452 and grew up in a small

7
00:01:11.651 --> 00:01:19.542
rural town near Florence, Italy. My parents weren't married, and I lived most of my childhood in my wealthy father's home with

8
00:01:19.543 --> 00:01:23.220
my grandparents and uncle. I spent a lot of time with my

9
00:01:23.221 --> 00:01:31.200
mother's family too. My childhood was wonderful; I roamed around the countryside, studying plants

10
00:01:31.201 --> 00:01:33.262
and animals. FAYE MOSS: Did

11
00:01:33.263 --> 00:01:40.045
you go to school? LEONARDO DA VINCI: I did not. I received some instruction in reading,

12
00:01:40.046 --> 00:01:44.907
writing, and a little basic math. Everything else I

13
00:01:44.908 --> 00:01:52.607
taught myself or learned from experience. In those days children whose parents weren't married didn't have the

14
00:01:52.608 --> 00:01:58.446
same educational opportunities as others. It worked out well for me, though, since it gave me a chance to

15
00:01:58.447 --> 00:02:04.108
pursue my own interests. I was a very curious child, and the world outside

16
00:02:04.109 --> 00:02:08.339
was my school! One time I happened upon a deep,

17
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:15.436
dark cave. I stood there astonished, torn between fear and curiosity about the things that might be

18
00:02:15.437 --> 00:02:19.722
inside. FAYE MOSS: Which won?

19
00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:25.777
Fear or curiosity? LEONARDO DA VINCI: Curiosity, of

20
00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:31.419
course! With me curiosity always won. And it paid off, for inside I found

21
00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.554
the fossil of a whale! Alas, I didn't

22
00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:45.262
stay in the countryside forever. I had already shown some interest in art, so when I was 14, my father took me to Florence to apprentice in

23
00:02:45.263 --> 00:02:53.681
an artisan's workshop. There I learned to paint, sculpt, and work with metals and leather from the master artisan Andrea

24
00:02:53.682 --> 00:03:04.390
del Verrocchio. His workshop was a lively place. Eventually I established a workshop to work on my own projects and train other young

25
00:03:04.391 --> 00:03:09.092
artists. FAYE MOSS: This seems like a good time to talk about

26
00:03:09.093 --> 00:03:20.188
your art. Your portrait of Lisa del Giocondo, famously known as the Mona Lisa , is probably the most famous painting in

27
00:03:20.189 --> 00:03:23.542
the world! LEONARDO DA VINCI: Oh, that?

28
00:03:23.543 --> 00:03:32.215
I never finished it! I kept tinkering with it and never gave it to the person who commissioned it, Francesco del

29
00:03:32.216 --> 00:03:38.915
Giocondo, Lisa's husband. Finally, I had some trouble with my hand and had to stop working on it before it

30
00:03:38.916 --> 00:03:48.038
was done. FAYE MOSS: I don't know what you could do to improve the Mona Lisa —it's already a masterpiece! And it

31
00:03:48.039 --> 00:03:54.886
looks so realistic! LEONARDO DA VINCI: In the painting I used a technique called sfumato , which

32
00:03:54.887 --> 00:04:04.339
is the Italian word for smoke . Using oil paints, I gently blended the colors to blur the lines. That's the

33
00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:10.999
way the human eye sees things. FAYE MOSS: Cool! Let's move on to another

34
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:22.028
famous painting of yours, The Last Supper . LEONARDO DA VINCI: It's much larger than the Mona Lisa —8.8 meters wide and 1.6 meters high,

35
00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:29.567
painted on the wall of a church dining room in Milan. Unlike the Mona Lisa , it's actually

36
00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:39.728
a finished painting, but my work on it progressed very slowly. On some days I painted all day long without stopping, but on other days I just

37
00:04:39.729 --> 00:04:44.677
stared at the painting, thinking about how to proceed. I'm afraid the

38
00:04:44.678 --> 00:04:49.334
pace of my work greatly frustrated the church's prior! The finished

39
00:04:49.335 --> 00:04:56.223
painting shows Jesus' last meal with his disciples. I must tell you, Faye, I struggled with how to depict the

40
00:04:56.224 --> 00:05:01.239
faces of Jesus and his betrayer, Judas. On the advice of a

41
00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:07.866
friend, I left Jesus' face incomplete. As for Judas—I may have used

42
00:05:07.867 --> 00:05:15.215
that annoying prior as the model! FAYE MOSS: From your paintings we can see that you were interested

43
00:05:15.216 --> 00:05:21.214
in things like light, perspective, and geometry. In fact, you kept notebooks filled

44
00:05:21.215 --> 00:05:30.404
with your thoughts, ideas, and plans. Your curiosity about all aspects of the world shines through. Was there

45
00:05:30.405 --> 00:05:34.015
anything you weren't interested in? LEONARDO DA

46
00:05:34.016 --> 00:05:44.581
VINCI: No. I was curious about it all! I dissected human bodies at hospitals, recorded observations about nature, and imagined futuristic

47
00:05:44.582 --> 00:05:53.712
things like robots and flying machines. But I didn't just dream of fantastic inventions. I also tried to figure out how they could be made

48
00:05:53.713 --> 00:06:03.631
and how they might work. FAYE MOSS: I'd love to read all of the brilliant entries in your notebooks, but your handwriting makes that .

49
00:06:03.632 --> 00:06:10.501
. . um, a little difficult! LEONARDO DA VINCI: Oh yes, I wrote

50
00:06:10.502 --> 00:06:17.724
backward, in mirrored handwriting, from right to left. I was left-handed, by the way, but exactly why I wrote

51
00:06:17.725 --> 00:06:22.652
in mirrored handwriting will remain my secret! Yet my notebooks might not be

52
00:06:22.653 --> 00:06:28.574
as interesting as you think, Faye. I wrote about ordinary things too, such as

53
00:06:28.575 --> 00:06:34.742
an inventory of my clothing. FAYE MOSS: That's how we know you

54
00:06:34.743 --> 00:06:41.312
liked to wear colorful clothing—like pink tights. I really approve of

55
00:06:41.313 --> 00:06:51.915
that fashion choice, by the way! Getting back to your life . . . Throughout your career you worked for many noble and rich families

56
00:06:51.916 --> 00:06:58.709
in Florence, Milan, and Rome. But in 1515, you left Italy for a

57
00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:09.067
special opportunity in France. LEONARDO DA VINCI: I moved there to join the service of King Francis, who bestowed upon me the title of Premier Painter,

58
00:07:09.068 --> 00:07:18.464
Engineer, and Architect to the King. The king generously gave me my own castle, and he visited me frequently to discuss plans and ideas that he wanted

59
00:07:18.465 --> 00:07:29.570
me to work on. Sadly, I was an old man by then and had limited use of my right hand. I regretted not having created more art earlier

60
00:07:29.571 --> 00:07:36.426
in my lifetime. FAYE MOSS: If it's any consolation, today your paintings are some of the most treasured

61
00:07:36.427 --> 00:07:40.715
masterpieces in the world. And you gave your genius to us

62
00:07:40.716 --> 00:07:47.280
with your notebooks. Thank you for sharing your life with us today. I'll let you get back to history

63
00:07:47.281 --> 00:07:52.275
now, Leonardo. LEONARDO DA VINCI:

64
00:07:52.276 --> 00:08:00.232
It was my pleasure, Faye. FAYE MOSS: Leonardo da Vinci's health deteriorated further while he

65
00:08:00.233 --> 00:08:08.678
was in France, leaving him bedridden. He died in 1519 at the age of 67 and was buried in a

66
00:08:08.679 --> 00:08:17.825
chapel within the royal castle. Leonardo accomplished so much during his lifetime that we were only able to talk about a small

67
00:08:17.826 --> 00:08:22.563
amount of it here. Today he is considered a genius for

68
00:08:22.564 --> 00:08:32.731
his beautiful artwork as well as his unique curiosity about subjects as widely ranging as military weapons, human anatomy,

69
00:08:32.732 --> 00:08:38.710
and architecture. Believe it or not, we're still learning new things about him and his

70
00:08:38.711 --> 00:08:50.821
works. I hope you enjoyed the show. I'll see you next time when we talk with another of history's greatest stars.

